<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Colombia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Republic of Colombia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Colombia is a country in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>, with insular regions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, bordered by Venezuela, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuador">Ecuador</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a>. Indigenous peoples lived there from at least 12,000 BCE. The Spanish landed in La Guajira in 1499 and by the mid-16th century had colonised much of the territory, establishing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>. Independence was declared in 1810, and after a Spanish reconquest it was secured in 1819, when the Gran Colombia period began. The state became the Granadine Confederation (1858), the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> of Colombia (1863) and a centralised republic in 1886. Panama seceded in 1903, which gave Colombia its present borders.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/colombia.jpg" alt="Colombia" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: See File history below for details. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Colombia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The territory served as a corridor of early human movement between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>, the Andes and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon</a> basin. The oldest finds, at Pubenza and El Totumo in the Magdalena Valley, date from the Paleoindian period (18,000–8000 BCE), and the oldest pottery found in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, at San Jacinto, dates to 5000–4000 BCE. The earliest gold craftsmanship is attributed to the Tumaco people of the Pacific coast, about 325 BCE, and the San Agustín culture raised ceremonial centres and stone monoliths between about 200 BCE and 800 CE.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alonso-de-ojeda">Alonso de Ojeda</a> reached the Guajira Peninsula in 1499, and Rodrigo de Bastidas explored the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> coast in 1500. Santa María la Antigua del Darién, founded in 1510, was the first stable settlement on the continent; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-marta">Santa Marta</a> followed in 1525 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cartagena-colombia">Cartagena</a> in 1533. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gonzalo-jimenez-de-quesada">Gonzalo Jiménez de Quesada</a> led an expedition into the interior in April 1536 and named the region the New Kingdom of Granada. In August 1538 he founded its capital, Santa Fe, near the Muisca cacicazgo of Muyquytá, which became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogota">Santa Fe de Bogotá</a>. The search for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-dorado">El Dorado</a> drew Sebastián de Belalcázar, who founded Cali and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/popayan">Popayán</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikolaus-federmann">Nikolaus Federmann</a>.</p><p>The Declaration of Independence was issued on 20 July 1810, now Independence Day, following the example of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a>'s independence in 1804. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a> became a leader of the movement, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-paula-santander">Francisco de Paula Santander</a> played a decisive part. The name Colombia, derived from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a> and intended to refer to the whole New World, was adopted by the Republic of 1819, formed from the territories of the Viceroyalty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-granada">New Granada</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> efforts to control the route of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama-canal">Panama Canal</a> contributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a>'s secession in 1903. In 1921 the United States paid Colombia $25,000,000 under the Thomson–Urrutia Treaty, and Colombia recognised Panama. A war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> over territory in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon</a> basin ended with a peace deal brokered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a>, which awarded the disputed area to Colombia in June 1934. An armed conflict among governments, paramilitaries and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a> such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolutionary-armed-forces-of-colombia">FARC</a> and the ELN began in the mid-1960s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Colombia is a presidential republic under the Constitution of 1991, divided into 32 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> and the Capital District of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogota">Bogotá</a>, its largest city. It covers 1,141,748 square kilometres and has a population of around 52 million. Spanish is the official language, with 65 Amerindian languages also used. The country spans the Andes, the Pacific and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> coasts, the Llanos plains and part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon rainforest</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Colombia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its official name was simply the Republic of <b>Colombia</b>; historians later coined ‘Gran Colombia’ to distinguish it from the present-day country.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gran-colombia">Gran Colombia · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>…less than a century the Incas united a larger area and population than any other state of the pre-Columbian Americas, stretching from southern <b>Colombia</b> to central Chile, and integrated coastal, highland and forest peoples into one administrative system without money, markets on a large scale or alphabetic writing.</blockquote><a href="/empire/inca-empire">Inca Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Muisca heartland became the core of the New Kingdom of Granada: Santa Fe de Bogotá, founded in 1538 on the savanna of the zipas, became the seat of a royal audiencia created in 1549, and later the capital of the Viceroyalty of New Granada and of <b>Colombia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/muisca-confederation">Muisca Confederation · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Andrés Bello, a Venezuelan scholar who settled in Chile, wrote its Civil Code of 1855, which was later adopted by Ecuador and <b>Colombia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-chile">Republic of Chile · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Rafael Núñez, the president behind the constitution of 1886, also wrote the words of <b>Colombia</b>’s national anthem, “¡Oh gloria inmarcesible!”, made official in 1920.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-colombia">Republic of Colombia · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The territory was then incorporated into Simón Bolívar’s Gran <b>Colombia</b> as its Southern District.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-ecuador">Republic of Ecuador · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
